room      prop_4
kind      equation
import    Newton, Principia, Andrew Motte trans., 1729 (Wikisource); Book I, Section II, Proposition IV.
caption   The centripetal forces of bodies, which by equoble motions describe different circles, tend to the centres of the same circles; and are one to the other, as the squares of the arcs described in equal times applied to the radii of the circles.
final     2

station 1
  ceiling   eq_force :: F \propto \dfrac{v^2}{r}
  gloss   The centripetal pull toward the centre grows with the square of the speed and shrinks with the radius.
  color   force #1f6feb
  color   speed #d1242f
  color   radius #953800
  color   nil #000000
  panel
    term    force   $F$
    term    nil     $\propto$
    term    speed   $v^2$  heart stabilo=#ffd6d6
    term    nil     $\big/$
    term    radius  $r$
    layout  ${force|$F$} \;\propto\; \dfrac{{speed|$v^2$}}{{radius|$r$}}$
  text
    The {force|centripetal force} — Newton's {force|pull toward the centre} — scales as the {speed|square of the speed} divided by the {radius|radius}. Double the {speed|speed} and the {force|pull} quadruples; double the {radius|radius} and the {force|pull} halves. Here the {speed|$v^2$} is the heart: it is the {speed|square}, not the speed itself, that governs how hard the body is drawn inward. Thus {force|$F$} answers to {speed|$v^2$} directly and to {radius|$r$} inversely.

station 2
  ceiling   eq_deriv :: F \propto \dfrac{\text{versed sine}}{t^2} \propto \dfrac{(\text{arc})^2}{D\,t^2} \propto \dfrac{v^2}{r}
  gloss   From versed sine and Lemma VII the pull resolves into the square of the speed over the radius.
  color   chord #d1242f
  color   force #1f6feb
  color   radius #953800
  color   nil #000000
  panel
    term    force   $F$
    term    nil     $\propto$
    term    chord   $\dfrac{\text{arc}^2}{D}$
    term    nil     $\Big/ t^2 \;\propto\;$
    term    chord   $v^2$  heart stabilo=#ffd6d6
    term    nil     $\big/$
    term    radius  $r$
    layout  ${force|$F$} \;\propto\; \dfrac{{chord|$v^2$}}{{radius|$r$}}$
  text
    By Prop. I and Cor. 4 the {force|centripetal force} in a least arc is as the versed sine of that arc taken in equal times. By Lemma VII the versed sine is as the {chord|arc squared} divided by the diameter, so {force|$F$} answers to {chord|$\text{arc}^2$}$/D$. Since the arc is as $v\,t$ and the diameter is as the {radius|radius}, the times cancel and the diameters give the {radius|$r$}, leaving the {chord|$v^2$} standing as the heart. The proof chain therefore closes on {force|$F$} $\propto$ {chord|$v^2$}$/${radius|$r$}.
